API-12: compute swmm_MAXROUTESTEP as the Courant step up to ROUTING_STEP, without side effects

getMaxRouteStep() called routing_getRoutingStep(RouteModel, MinRouteStep)
with the Courant factor set to 1. The second argument is the upper limit of
the search, and the result is clamped to at least MinRouteStep, so the value
was always MinRouteStep (0.5 s by default). The call also went through
dynwave_getRoutingStep(), which overwrites the solver's VariableStep (a call
between swmm_start and the first swmm_step changed the first step from
MinRouteStep to the full Courant step) and counts time-step critical
elements. Add dynwave_getMaxRoutingStep(), which runs the same link and node
step checks with a Courant factor of 1, capped at ROUTING_STEP and clamped
and rounded as the solver does, but changes no state, and use it.

Apply from the root of a Stormwater-Management-Model checkout at cb3e192b:
    git apply API-12_swmm530.patch

diff --git a/src/legacy/engine/dynwave.c b/src/legacy/engine/dynwave.c
index 972da8c..4687ea5 100644
--- a/src/legacy/engine/dynwave.c
+++ b/src/legacy/engine/dynwave.c
@@ -211,6 +211,26 @@ double dynwave_getRoutingStep(double fixedStep)
     return VariableStep;
 }
 
+/*!
+* \brief Finds the largest stable time step for the current flow solution
+*        (Courant factor of 1) without changing the solver's state.
+* \param[in] maxStep Upper limit on the time step (sec)
+* \return Returns time step (sec)
+*/
+double dynwave_getMaxRoutingStep(double maxStep)
+{
+    int    minLink = -1, minNode = -1;
+    double saveCourantFactor = CourantFactor;
+    double tMin;
+
+    CourantFactor = 1.0;
+    tMin = getLinkStep(maxStep, &minLink);
+    tMin = getNodeStep(tMin, &minNode);
+    CourantFactor = saveCourantFactor;
+    if ( tMin < MinRouteStep ) tMin = MinRouteStep;
+    return floor(1000.0 * tMin) / 1000.0;
+}
+
